\title{ptmlink: query the PTMlink database from R}
\description{
PTMlink ranks pairs of one N-glycosylation site and one phosphorylation site by how strongly the two co-vary
across CPTAC tumor cohorts. The package reads the same data as the PTMlink website and needs no setup:
\verb{library(ptmlink); ptm_gene("EGFR")}.
}
\section{Where to start}{

\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=ptm_status]{ptm_status()}} says how a gene name resolves and what was measured for it.
\item \code{\link[=ptm_gene]{ptm_gene()}} and \code{\link[=ptm_site]{ptm_site()}} list the ranked partners of a gene or a site.
\item \code{\link[=ptm_pair]{ptm_pair()}} gives one pair with its estimate in every cohort; \code{\link[=ptm_explain]{ptm_explain()}} gives the cautions that apply.
\item \code{\link[=ptm_network]{ptm_network()}} and \code{\link[=ptm_plot_network]{ptm_plot_network()}} give the partners of a gene as a network.
\item \code{\link[=ptm_query]{ptm_query()}} and \code{\link[=ptm_list]{ptm_list()}} read the full table of reported pairs, which is released with the article.
\item \code{\link[=ptm_columns]{ptm_columns()}} defines every column; \code{\link[=ptm_default_filters]{ptm_default_filters()}} describes the default view; \code{\link[=ptm_cite]{ptm_cite()}} says how to cite.
}
